# THE SIFREI ROWS OF PISKAOT 132-136 (THE DEUTERONOMY WALK sitting 14 — CHAPTER 16, LEAN, 2026-09-23): the Passover not in one of your gates — the party of
# one, the majority, the private altar (132), the evening, the sunset and the season — two teachers (133), the cooking, the overnight stay, the minor Passover,
# six days against seven (134), the seventh day's assembly — the study house and the table, the intermediate days given to the sages (135, HEADLESS), the
# weeks counted by the court and by each man, from the sickle, by night (136). Every row read whole; the cuts by consonants (ch16_ink.SP_). Chapter 15's form.
from ch16_ink import *
S = {}
def srow(p, r, verdict, prose): S[(p, r)] = (verdict, prose)

srow(132, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"YOU MAY NOT SLAUGHTER THE PASSOVER WITHIN ONE OF YOUR GATES" (16:5) — R. JUDAH SAYS: WHENCE THAT ONE WHO SLAUGHTERS THE PASSOVER FOR AN INDIVIDUAL TRANSGRESSES A PROHIBITION? THE TEACHING SAYS "YOU MAY NOT SLAUGHTER THE PASSOVER FOR ONE". The head row of piska 132 ({SP_(132, 1, "לשוחט", "את", "הפסח", "על", "היחיד")} "one who slaughters the Passover for the individual"): "ONE" READ AS THE PARTY\'S SIZE, not the gate\'s — the parser\'s [1] at 16:5 the word the row turns (Mishnah Pesachim 8:7 the exam): the compile\'s party parameter, a dispute. Fresh.')
srow(132, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'R. YOSE SAYS: SOMETIMES HE IS ONE AND THEY SLAUGHTER IT FOR HIM, SOMETIMES THEY ARE TEN AND THEY DO NOT — ONE WHO CAN EAT IT, YES; TEN WHO CANNOT, NO, LEST THEY BRING THE PASSOVER TO DISQUALIFICATION. The row without a citation ({SP_(132, 2, "אחד", "ויכול", "לאכלו")} "one who can eat it"; {SP_(132, 2, "לידי", "פסול")} "to disqualification"): THE PARTY\'S MEASURE THE EATING, not the count — R. Judah refused; the leftover (131:4) the ground. Fresh.')
srow(132, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ELEAZAR BEN MATTITHIAH SAYS: SINCE THE COMMUNITY OFFERS THE PASSOVER IN UNCLEANNESS WHEN THE MAJORITY ARE UNCLEAN, MIGHT AN INDIVIDUAL TIP THE BALANCE? THE TEACHING SAYS "YOU MAY NOT SLAUGHTER THE PASSOVER FOR ONE". ({SP_(132, 3, "שהצבור", "עושים", "פסח", "בטמאה")} "that the community makes the Passover in uncleanness"; {SP_(132, 3, "יחיד", "מכריע")} "an individual tipping the balance"): the verse cut short of "your gates" to read "one" as the tie-breaker (the English\'s note): THE MAJORITY RULE for the unclean Passover (Numbers 9\'s second Passover the kin) — an individual does not decide it; Tosefta Pesachim 6:2. Fresh.')
srow(132, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'R. SIMEON SAYS: ONE WHO SLAUGHTERS THE PASSOVER FOR AN INDIVIDUAL ON A PRIVATE ALTAR WHEN THE ALTARS ARE FORBIDDEN TRANSGRESSES; MIGHT IT BE SO WHEN THEY ARE PERMITTED? "IN ONE OF YOUR GATES" — WHEN ISRAEL IS GATHERED IN ONE PLACE, WHEN THE ALTARS ARE FORBIDDEN, NOT WHEN PERMITTED. ({SP_(132, 4, "בבמת", "יחיד", "בשעת", "אסור", "הבמה")} "on a private altar at the hour of the altar\'s ban"; {SP_(132, 4, "ולא", "בשעת", "התר", "הבמה")} "and not at the hour of the altar\'s permission"): THE ERAS TABLE AT 16:5 — the ban holds in the eras of one place (Shiloh, Jerusalem), not in the eras of permitted altars (chapter 12\'s stations, the cell reused); Tosefta Zevachim 13:16. Fresh.')
srow(133, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"BUT AT THE PLACE WHICH THE LORD YOUR GOD WILL CHOOSE" (16:6) — R. ELIEZER SAYS: AT EVENING YOU SLAUGHTER, AT THE GOING DOWN OF THE SUN YOU EAT, AT THE SEASON OF YOUR GOING OUT OF EGYPT YOU BURN. The head row of piska 133 ({SP_(133, 1, "בערב", "אתה", "זובח")} "at evening you slaughter"; {SP_(133, 1, "כבוא", "השמש", "אתה", "אוכל")} "at the going down of the sun you eat"; {SP_(133, 1, "אתה", "שורף")} "you burn"): THE THREE TIMES OF 16:6 MAPPED TO THREE ACTS — slaughter, eating, burning (the morning after): "between the evenings" nowhere in the chapter (asserted) — the clock read from this verse\'s three phrases; the compile\'s Passover clock (afternoon, night, morning). Fresh.')
srow(133, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'R. AKIVA SAYS: AT EVENING YOU SLAUGHTER, AT THE GOING DOWN OF THE SUN YOU EAT — UNTIL WHEN? UNTIL THE SEASON OF YOUR GOING OUT. ANOTHER WORD: THE SEASON OF YOUR GOING OUT FOR ITS SLAUGHTER, THE GOING DOWN OF THE SUN FOR ITS ROASTING, THE EVENING FOR ITS EATING. ({SP_(133, 2, "עד", "מתי", "עד", "מועד", "צאתך", "ממצרים")} "until when? until the season of your going out of Egypt"; {SP_(133, 2, "כבוא", "השמש", "לצליתו")} "the going down of the sun for its roasting"): THE DISPUTE ON THE THIRD PHRASE — R. Akiva\'s the eating\'s END (till morning), the other word the roasting: the eating window\'s close a parameter (Mishnah Pesachim 10:9 / Zevachim 5:8 "until midnight" the exam; Berakhot 9a the segment if a cell needs it). Fresh.')
srow(134, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"YOU SHALL COOK AND EAT IT IN THE PLACE WHICH THE LORD YOUR GOD WILL CHOOSE" (16:7) — IT TEACHES THAT HE MAY RETURN THE WHOLE BUT NOT THE PIECES AND THE LIMBS, R. JUDAH\'S WORDS; R. YOSE SAYS: ON THE SABBATH EITHER WAY FORBIDDEN, ON THE FESTIVAL EITHER WAY PERMITTED. The head row of piska 134 ({SP_(134, 1, "את", "החתיכות", "ואת", "האברים")} "the pieces and the limbs"; {SP_(134, 1, "בשבת", "בין", "כך", "ובין", "כך", "אסור")} "on the Sabbath either way forbidden"): "COOK" READ AS ROAST-AND-RETURN — the verb\'s contradiction with Exodus 12:9 (asserted: nothing shared in order; the Chronicler\'s "boiled with fire") not raised here, the row on returning the roast to the fire on the Sabbath; Tosefta Pesachim 7:2. Fresh.')
srow(134, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL TURN IN THE MORNING AND GO TO YOUR TENTS" — IT TEACHES THAT THEY REQUIRE AN OVERNIGHT STAY. ({SP_(134, 2, "שטעונים", "לינה")} "that they require an overnight stay"): THE NIGHT IN JERUSALEM an effect of the pilgrimage — "in the morning" the earliest departure; the compile\'s stay parameter. Fresh.')
srow(134, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'I HAVE ONLY THESE; WHENCE TO INCLUDE BIRDS, MEAL OFFERINGS, WINE, FRANKINCENSE AND WOOD? "AND YOU SHALL TURN" — EVERY TURNING YOU TURN, FROM THE MORNING ONWARD. ({SP_(134, 3, "עופות", "ומנחות", "יין", "ולבונה", "ועצים")} "birds and meal offerings, wine and frankincense and wood"; {SP_(134, 3, "כל", "פונות", "שאתה", "פונה")} "every turning that you turn"): the stay extended to every offering\'s bringer. Fresh.')
srow(134, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'R. JUDAH SAYS: MIGHT THE MINOR PASSOVER REQUIRE THE STAY? "YOU SHALL TURN IN THE MORNING … SIX DAYS YOU SHALL EAT UNLEAVENED BREAD" — WHAT REQUIRES SIX REQUIRES THE STAY; THE MINOR PASSOVER, ONE DAY, WENT OUT. THE SAGES: IT IS LIKE THE WOOD AND THE FRANKINCENSE, WHICH REQUIRE THE STAY. ({SP_(134, 4, "פסח", "קטן", "טעון", "לינה")} "the minor Passover requires the stay"; {SP_(134, 4, "את", "שטעון", "ששה", "טעון", "לינה")} "what requires six requires the stay"): THE SECOND PASSOVER (Numbers 9, the kin\'s ledger) at 16:8\'s "six days" — a dispute; 16:8 read across the verse edge with 16:7. Fresh.')
srow(134, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'"SIX DAYS YOU SHALL EAT UNLEAVENED BREAD" (16:8) — R. SIMEON SAYS: ONE VERSE SAYS SIX DAYS, ANOTHER (EXODUS 12:15) SAYS SEVEN DAYS — UNLEAVENED BREAD IS EATEN ALL SEVEN: SIX FROM THE NEW GRAIN AND ON THE SEVENTH FROM THE OLD. ({SP_(134, 5, "כתוב", "אחד", "אומר", "ששת", "ימים")} "one verse says six days"; {SP_(134, 5, "ששה", "מן", "החדש", "ובשביעי", "מן", "הישן")} "six from the new and on the seventh from the old"): I13\'S QUESTION WITHOUT ITS THIRD VERSE (checked) — SIX AGAINST SEVEN upheld by the omer: the new grain permitted from the second day (16:9\'s sickle), so six days of the new: the compile\'s two arms (the days\' count seven, the new grain\'s six); the diff asserted two tokens. Fresh.')
srow(135, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND ON THE SEVENTH DAY A SOLEMN ASSEMBLY TO THE LORD YOUR GOD" (16:8) — RABBI SAYS: MIGHT A MAN BE SHUT UP IN THE STUDY HOUSE THE WHOLE DAY? THE TEACHING SAYS "FOR YOU" (NUMBERS 29:35). MIGHT HE EAT AND DRINK THE WHOLE DAY? "A SOLEMN ASSEMBLY TO THE LORD". HOW THEN? GIVE A PART TO THE STUDY HOUSE AND A PART TO EATING AND DRINKING. The head row of the HEADLESS piska 135 — its first citation Numbers 29:35, not at its head ({SP_(135, 1, "עצור", "בבית", "המדרש", "כל", "היום", "כלו")} "shut up in the study house the whole day"; {SP_(135, 1, "תן", "חלק", "לבית", "המדרש")} "give a part to the study house"): THE FESTIVAL DAY HALF AND HALF — "to the LORD" here against "for you" at the other assembly (Numbers 29:35, the kin\'s ledger): the two verses read together as one rule (Pesachim 68b / Beitzah 15b the segment). Fresh.')
srow(135, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ISHMAEL SAYS: SINCE WE HAVE NOT LEARNED THAT THE INTERMEDIATE DAYS ARE FORBIDDEN FOR WORK, WHENCE? "SIX DAYS YOU SHALL EAT UNLEAVENED BREAD AND ON THE SEVENTH A SOLEMN ASSEMBLY" — AS THE SEVENTH IS RESTRAINED, SO THE SIXTH IS RESTRAINED. ({SP_(135, 2, "שימי", "מועד", "אסורים", "במלאכה")} "that the intermediate days are forbidden for work"; {SP_(135, 2, "מה", "שביעי", "עצור", "אף", "ששי", "עצור")} "as the seventh is restrained so the sixth is restrained"): THE INTERMEDIATE DAYS\' WORK BAN drawn from the seventh — the first step. Fresh.')
srow(135, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'OR AS THE SEVENTH IS RESTRAINED FROM ALL WORK, SO THE SIXTH? THE TEACHING SAYS "SIX DAYS … AND ON THE SEVENTH A SOLEMN ASSEMBLY" — THE SEVENTH FROM ALL WORK, THE SIXTH NOT FROM ALL WORK: SCRIPTURE HANDED IT TO THE SAGES TO SAY WHICH DAY IS FORBIDDEN AND WHICH PERMITTED, WHICH WORK FORBIDDEN AND WHICH PERMITTED. ({SP_(135, 3, "שביעי", "עצור", "מכל", "מלאכה", "ואין", "ששי", "עצור", "מכל", "מלאכה")} "the seventh restrained from all work, and the sixth not restrained from all work"; {SP_(135, 3, "לא", "מסרו", "הכתוב", "אלא", "לחכמים")} "Scripture handed it only to the sages"): THE ORAL LAW\'S SEAT NAMED IN THE HEBREW — the intermediate days\' work a PARAMETER Scripture gives the sages to fill (Mishnah Moed Katan the answer sheet; Chagigah 18a the segment): the code/data law\'s cleanest case in the chapter — the verse installs the bar, the sages\' table its content. Fresh.')
srow(136, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"SEVEN WEEKS YOU SHALL COUNT FOR YOURSELF" (16:9) — IN THE COURT. WHENCE FOR EACH AND EVERY ONE? "AND YOU SHALL COUNT FOR YOURSELVES FROM THE MORROW OF THE SABBATH" (LEVITICUS 23:15) — EACH AND EVERY ONE. The head row of piska 136 ({SP_(136, 1, "תספר", "לך", "בבית", "דין")} "you shall count for yourself — in the court"; {SP_(136, 1, "כל", "אחד", "ואחד")} "each and every one"): THE TWO COUNTS — the court\'s from the singular "for yourself", every man\'s from Leviticus\'s plural (the kin\'s ledger); the compile: the omer count an effect on the calendar (the court\'s) and on each person (Menachot 65b-66a the segment if needed). Fresh.')
srow(136, 2, 'CONTEXT', f'"FROM WHEN THE SICKLE BEGINS ON THE STANDING GRAIN" — FROM WHEN THE SICKLE BEGINS ON THE STANDING GRAIN, ({SP_(136, 2, "משתתחיל", "חרמש", "בקמה")} "from when the sickle begins on the standing grain"): the phrase restated to be parsed by the next row. Fresh.')
srow(136, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'THAT IT ALL BE STANDING; THAT IT BE THE FIRST OF ALL THAT IS REAPED; THAT ITS REAPING BE ONLY WITH A SICKLE. The row without a citation ({SP_(136, 3, "שיהא", "הכל", "בקמה")} "that it all be standing"; {SP_(136, 3, "אלא", "בחרמש")} "only with a sickle"): THE OMER\'S THREE RULES FROM THREE WORDS — standing, first, sickle (the sickle\'s two Bible seats asserted: here and 23:26); Mishnah Menachot 10:8-9 the exam. Fresh.')
srow(136, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'MIGHT HE REAP AND BRING, AND COUNT WHENEVER HE WISHES? THE TEACHING SAYS "FROM WHEN THE SICKLE BEGINS ON THE STANDING GRAIN YOU SHALL BEGIN TO COUNT". ({SP_(136, 4, "ויספור", "כל", "זמן", "שירצה")} "and count whenever he wishes"): the count bound to the reaping\'s hour. Fresh.')
srow(136, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'MIGHT HE REAP AND COUNT, AND BRING WHENEVER HE WISHES? THE TEACHING SAYS "FROM THE DAY YOU BRING … YOU SHALL COUNT" (LEVITICUS 23:15). ({SP_(136, 5, "ויביא", "כל", "זמן", "שירצה")} "and bring whenever he wishes"): the bringing bound to the count — Leviticus\'s verse completes this one (the kin\'s ledger). Fresh.')
srow(136, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'MIGHT HE REAP BY DAY, COUNT BY DAY AND BRING BY DAY? THE TEACHING SAYS "SEVEN COMPLETE SABBATHS THEY SHALL BE" (LEVITICUS 23:15) — WHEN ARE THEY COMPLETE? WHEN YOU BEGIN FROM THE EVENING. ({SP_(136, 6, "אימתי", "הן", "תמימות")} "when are they complete"; {SP_(136, 6, "משאתה", "מתחיל", "מבערב")} "from when you begin from the evening"): THE COUNT BEGINS AT NIGHT — "complete" weeks read as evening-to-evening. Fresh.')
srow(136, 7, 'MATERIAL', f'MIGHT HE REAP BY NIGHT, COUNT BY NIGHT AND BRING BY NIGHT? "FROM THE DAY YOU BRING" — THERE IS NO BRINGING BUT BY DAY. ({SP_(136, 7, "אין", "הבאה", "אלא", "ביום")} "there is no bringing but by day"): the bringing by day. Fresh.')
srow(136, 8, 'MATERIAL', f'HOW THEN? REAPING AND COUNTING BY NIGHT, AND BRINGING BY DAY. The row without a citation ({SP_(136, 8, "קצירה", "וספירה", "בלילה", "והבאה", "ביום")} "reaping and counting by night, and bringing by day"): THE OMER\'S SCHEDULE RESOLVED — the compile\'s three clock seats (the reaping the night after the first day, the count from that night, the bringing the next day); Mishnah Menachot 10:3 the exam; the Sifra\'s parallel (the English\'s note). Fresh.')
